WHY THIS WORKS
- treats the partner as a person with a reasonable position, not an obstacle
- gives her a reason to bring him, not a technique for managing him
- arc 2 accepts a no from the household without working around it
- no line about the program being paid, because the cost is her husband's stated objection. that is the only reason it can be left out

CONVERSATION 1: he thinks it is another expense

Lead: I want to do this but my husband thinks it's another thing we'll spend money on that doesn't work. We've already spent a lot.

Sonia: That's a reasonable position from where he's standing, especially if the previous spends came with promises.

What have the previous ones been?

Lead: Two IUIs and a nutritionist who gave me a meal plan I couldn't stick to.

Sonia: Then his scepticism has been earned, not invented, and I'd rather he brought it to the call than sat outside it.

Lead: He'd probably just say no if I asked him to come.

Sonia: What would he say no to specifically? The cost, or being sold to for an hour?

Lead: The second one mostly. He hates that stuff.

Sonia: Then tell him the call is my team working out whether I can help, and that they'll say so if I can't. He can come and be sceptical the whole way through, that's genuinely fine.

Lead: He'd actually respect that framing.

Sonia: Most people in his position do. The pitch is what makes them dig in.

The other thing worth saying: he'd be part of this, not a spectator. If there's a male factor at all, half the work is his.

Lead: He'd like having a job in it. He hates feeling useless.

Sonia: Most men do. So bring him.

